exercises.reference:
AW = {"H": 1.008, "C": 12.011, "N": 14.007, "O": 15.999, "Fe": 55.845}

def empirical_formula(percent):
    """Smallest whole-number formula from mass percentages, e.g. {'C': 1, 'H': 2}."""
    moles = {el: p / AW[el] for el, p in percent.items()}
    smallest = min(moles.values())
    ratio = {el: n / smallest for el, n in moles.items()}
    for k in range(1, 11):
        if all(abs(r * k - round(r * k)) < 0.05 for r in ratio.values()):
            return {el: round(r * k) for el, r in ratio.items()}
    raise ValueError("no small whole-number ratio found")

exercises.solution:
Percentages are grams per 100 g, so dividing by atomic weights gives moles per 100 g. Dividing by the smallest amount gives ratios such as 1 : 1.333; then try multipliers until every ratio is (nearly) whole:

```python
def empirical_formula(percent):
    moles = {el: p / AW[el] for el, p in percent.items()}
    smallest = min(moles.values())
    ratio = {el: n / smallest for el, n in moles.items()}
    for k in range(1, 11):
        if all(abs(r * k - round(r * k)) < 0.05 for r in ratio.values()):
            return {el: round(r * k) for el, r in ratio.items()}
    raise ValueError("no small whole-number ratio found")
```

For magnetite the ratio Fe : O is 1 : 1.333; multiplying by 3 gives Fe₃O₄. For N₂O₅ it is 1 : 2.50, which needs a multiplier of 2. Rounding 1.333 to 1, or 2.5 to 2 or 3, would give a wrong formula, which is why the loop over multipliers matters.

